# collatz-in: the trajectory of whatever number you give it
#
#     $ echo 27 | vonal run examples/collatz-in.vsr
#
# Identical to collatz.vsr but for one cell. Its seed at (0,0) was a disc
# pushing the literal 6; here it is a cross reading a number from stdin, so
# the plate computes from input rather than from a constant baked into its
# own picture. Every other cell, including the whole loop body, is unchanged.
#
# The horizontal swell puts scale 0 at column 0, and a cross ignores its
# scale anyway, so the substitution costs the composition nothing.
#
# Termination is the Collatz conjecture's problem, not this plate's: it halts
# for any positive integer anyone has ever tried, and loops forever on 0, on
# a negative number, and on end-of-input (where IN_NUM pushes -1). Use
# --max-steps if you want a guaranteed stop.
#
# Row layout, the loop body and the odd branch are all documented in
# collatz.vsr, which this is a copy of.
#
# Ends with a newline: push 1, push_acc 2 makes 10, then OUT_CHAR.
%plate 18x3

+3.5  v1.5  o125  o125  o135  o145  o155  o165  o175  o175  o165  o155  o145  o135  o125  o125  o115  o1.5
>1.5  >1.5  >1.5  %1.5  +1.5  o145  o2.5  +2.5  %1.5  o115  D3.5  v2.5  %1.5  o125  #5.5  v2.5  o125  #4.5
o115  #1.5  ^1.5  o125  o135  o145  o155  ...5  +2.5  o225  o115  <1.5  o145  o135  o125  >1.5  o135  #3.5
